{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Popper's preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on his own terms not as an inductive inference of future success, but as a non-inductive methodological rule aligned with the aim of science (verisimilitude) and a pragmatic decision to act on the theory that has withstood the most severe attempts at falsification, acknowledging that this preference carries no guarantee of truth or future survival.","counterconditions":["If verisimilitude cannot be defined without probabilistic assumptions (as argued by Tichý and Miller), then the appeal to truth-likeness collapses into the very induction Popper rejects.","If the stability of test conditions in the future requires an inductive assumption about the uniformity of nature, then the preference for action smuggles in induction despite methodological denials.","If 'rational preference' implicitly assumes that past survival is a reliable guide to future utility, then the distinction between psychological expectation and logical justification is merely semantic."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The argument proceeds by distinguishing between 'justification' (which implies probability/induction) and 'rational preference' (which implies alignment with goals). It posits that while corroboration reports only past performance, the rational agent whose goal is truth (or high verisimilitude) prefers the theory that has survived severe tests because it is the only one not known to be false, whereas competitors may have been eliminated. This relies on the concept of 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) as a regulative ideal rather than a calculable probability, and frames the choice as a 'critical preference' rather than an 'inductive expectation'.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}